  "configuration_id": "config_018",
  "reason": "Evidence: Adding the image modality (resnet50) to the RNA-only baseline improved accuracy by ~4.2% (0.485→0.527), confirming multimodal fusion is beneficial. The concat vs weighted fusion difference is negligible (~0.3%), so fusion method is not the key lever. The largest untested architectural dimension is profile representation: all three completed experiments use singleprofile, yet multiprofile with aggregation could capture richer cellular state information. Question: Does aggregating multiple profiles (via avgpool) in the multimodal setting improve over the singleprofile baseline, while holding RNA encoder (pca_n2000), image encoder (resnet50), and fusion (concat) constant at the values from our best result (config_016)? Why this is the best next experiment: It isolates the effect of multiprofile+aggregation as the only changed variable relative to config_016, making the comparison clean and informative. Avgpool is a conservative, low-overfitting-risk aggregation that tests whether the multiprofile signal is present at all before committing to a more complex transformer aggregation. If multiprofile helps, we then know to invest in better aggregation; if it doesn't, we save budget by avoiding transformer experiments. This maximizes information gain per experiment under the limited budget."
}

